  <h2>Audience Q&amp;A: MMR, Research, and Replication</h2>
  <p>And now it's time for you to ask questions about what matters to you and your family.</p>
  <p>Uh, so who's first? Raise your hand. We'll bring you microphone.</p>
  <p>Stand up, uh, in fourth row. Yes, you. The one with your hand up. There you go.</p>
  <p>Uh, say your first name only and state your question or comment. Go.</p>
  <p>Uh, I'm Kendall and my question is could you explain the process?</p>
  <p>I saw the movie Vaxxed where kids would regress into autism after their 2-year MMR shot.</p>
  <p>Can you explain the process from the ingredients in the MMR to when it goes in your arm to the encephalitis—brain inflammation—and how it actually causes autism?</p>
  <p>Can you just kind of explain it in a way that everyone can understand?</p>
  <p>Well, right now we're in the process of researching all those questions and that's something because it's so often reported by parents and physicians— that chain of events—where somebody goes in for their, you know, 16-month or wellness visit and they get the MMR and maybe a number of other vaccines at the same time.</p>
  <p>And many of the parents have reported that their child developed autism immediately after the vaccine.</p>
  <p>So, and that's something that we're looking at right now.</p>
  <p>Unfortunately, the problem is that those kind of studies that should have been done on that question a long time ago were never done.</p>
  <p>Instead, there was a series of epidemiological studies that were created by, uh, by I would say captured researchers at CDC, mainly people who work for the pharmaceutical industry.</p>
  <p>And they produce very bad studies.</p>
  <p>None of those studies did the one thing that you would want to do in an epidemiological study, which is study outcomes in an unvaccinated group and health outcomes in a vaccinated group.</p>
  <p>And no study like that was done by any government agency.</p>
  <p>And it was deliberate that it wasn't done.</p>
  <p>But that's one of the things that you're studying now with gold standard science.</p>
  <p>We are now—</p>
  <p>Yeah. And we're doing gold standard science which means replication, as you pointed out.</p>
  <p>At this point there's no program at NIH that requires replication. We've changed that.</p>
  <p>So we're going to put about 20% of our budget into replicating studies.</p>
  <p>And that's, you know, what we mean by replication.</p>
  <p>That means one independent group does a study and they get a result.</p>
  <p>Then somebody that's totally independent of them has to do exactly the same study with the same parameters and come up with the same result and the same data sets.</p>
  <p>Yeah, the same data sets or it doesn't count.</p>

  <h2>Audience Q&amp;A: Big Pharma Influence and Advertising</h2>
  <p>All right. Where's Cheryl? 257. Cheryl. Cheryl. Cheryl. Huh?</p>
  <p>Excuse me. I'm Cheryl and I'm a nurse for 30 years. I quit in 2021.</p>
  <p>How do you propose to rein in big pharma and the influence they exert over the health and well-being of Americans?</p>
  <p>Um, that's a good question and we are, I would say, to use your words, laser focused on that.</p>
  <p>And probably the biggest—you know—there are a number of dynamics that account for the capture of agencies by the industries they're supposed to regulate.</p>
  <p>This process by which the agency becomes almost like a trade association for the industry it regulates—a sock puppet, I would say.</p>
  <p>That's how CDC and NIH and FDA have functioned in the past.</p>
  <p>And one of the ways that happens—one of the really potent contributors to that—is the revolving door.</p>
  <p>I think seven out of the last eight FDA commissioners have gone immediately to work for pharmaceutical companies.</p>
  <p>That longest-reigning CDC director, Julie Gerberding, who did several billion dollars' worth of favors for Merck, became the vice president of Merck's vaccine division.</p>
  <p>And this is true throughout not just the top level of the agency, but every level of the agency.</p>
  <p>And we're going to end that so that people cannot go to work for the industry that they've been regulating.</p>
  <p>Are you against direct-to-consumer advertising?</p>
  <p>I think that's a huge mistake and we're looking at ways now to end that.</p>
  <p>Um, but I'll say very briefly, one of the probably the most potent mechanisms that pharma has is that pharma controls the panels.</p>
  <p>New drugs are approved by outside panels, not by FDA or CDC.</p>
  <p>They're panels within them that are made up of experts.</p>
  <p>Well, those experts are supposed to be independent experts, but they're not. They work for the drug companies, almost all of them.</p>
  <p>And so they have a vested interest. They have real conflict of interest in rubber stamping new drugs, new vaccines because they know that they have a vaccine in the pipeline that they're going to expect their cronies to rubber stamp.</p>
  <p>And so, you know, these things are just greenlighted, flagged through the process with no demand for safety studies.</p>
  <p>There's never been a pre-licensing safety study for any vaccine that is a double-blind trial against a placebo.</p>
  <p>And so, and that's insane.</p>
  <p>You're mandating this product. It's a liability-free product.</p>
  <p>You can't sue them if they kill you or they injure you, no matter how reckless or negligent they are.</p>
  <p>And there's no safety studies at the outset.</p>
  <p>There's no surveillance system afterward.</p>
  <p>And you're mandating that product for 76 million American children and we don't know anything about it.</p>
  <p>So, we're changing those panels so that they're going to be independent scientists.</p>
  <p>And we're going to make sure that every new product is adequately tested both for efficacy and for safety.</p>
  <p>That's so good.</p>
  <p>All right. Who else? Stand up. Stand up. We'll bring you a mic right quick. Let's go. Go. Speed.</p>
  <p>I'm Nicole.</p>
  <p>You had addressed a couple of things just a bit ago about years ago being the tobacco industry obviously being huge and at one point, uh, you know, cigarettes and stuff were being advertised like crazy and then it got banned where you don't see those commercials anymore.</p>
  <p>Well, how can we ever get these pharmaceutical companies to stop advertising when you just said it's 75% of a lot of times the money?</p>
  <p>But who wants to hear they're harming—you know, it talks about your leg's going to fall off, your eyeball might fall off, you know, all the side effects you get from it.</p>
  <p>That's not—we want to hear like good products that we can buy, not pharmaceutical.</p>
  <p>That's what we were just talking about—direct-to-consumer—because the consumer can't buy the drug. They got to get it from the doctor.</p>
  <p>But you're telling it to the consumer so they'll call their doctor and say, "I want that drug."</p>
  <p>Right. And that's what you want to stop.</p>
  <p>Yeah.</p>
  <p>And you know, with the tobacco and with alcohol—which alcohol, when I was a kid, you would not see any alcohol ads on television.</p>
  <p>Now you're starting to—then you started to see them when cable TV because they didn't sign on to the agreement.</p>
  <p>And it was a voluntary agreement under threat that they were going to mandate it.</p>
  <p>And my father was actually involved in both the ban on cigarettes and alcohol when he was in the United States Senate.</p>
  <p>Um, there is a very bad Supreme Court decision on free speech and commercial advertising.</p>
  <p>Free speech under the Constitution is political speech and there are many cases that said it doesn't apply to political advertising, that you can do some regulation of political or commercial advertising.</p>
  <p>At the Supreme Court decision when the last Trump administration tried to change the drug ads to make them report the price of the drug, the Supreme Court said you can't do that.</p>
  <p>Now we think we have other arguments and one of those arguments—the pharmaceutical ads are different than any other ads.</p>
  <p>Number one, they are advertising a product that the taxpayer is going to have to pay for.</p>
  <p>If you advertise cigarettes or beer, you're buying it yourself and you're making that choice.</p>
  <p>But when you buy a pharmaceutical drug, my agency in most cases is going to have to pay for it.</p>
  <p>So it's the taxpayers are paying for it. So it's a very unusual product in that way.</p>
  <p>Yeah.</p>
  <p>The other thing is that the government—they're getting a tax write-off from that ad.</p>
  <p>So the taxpayers are paying for the ad and then the person who gets that product is billing the government for it.</p>
  <p>And so it's a very different—we think it's so generous that it has arguments that, um, you know, that we think—and we're looking at this now with the White House—the ways that we can get by that Supreme Court decision.</p>

